826:
It shall be unlawful for any person or persons to discharge any firearms including, but not limited to, rifles, pistols, shotguns, pellet guns, dynamite caps or other explosives within the corporate limits of the city unless:
In defense of person, property or habitation;
By any police officer engaged in the discharge of his official duties;
By persons engaged in military duties or exercises;
With written permission of the mayor and council of the city.
(Comp. Ords. 2005, § 6-109)

844:
Before accepting a plea to an offense charged, the court shall in all cases, in language in which defendant can understand, inform him of the nature of the charge against him and the penalty which may be imposed upon conviction, and shall inform the defendant of his fundamental constitutional rights. The fundamental constitutional rights should include, but not be limited to, the following:
He is not required to make any statement and that anything said by him may be used against him;
He may have a lawyer;
He may have his case delayed to give him sufficient time to be advised by a lawyer;
He may have a lawyer present during any questioning and proceeding;
If he is without means to employ a lawyer, his case will be bound over to the appropriate authority that has the means to appoint a lawyer for his behalf;
He may or may not testify in his own behalf;
He may appeal any final decision rendered by the court;
A record of any conviction will become a part of his permanent record;
He may be admitted to bail in appropriate cases;
He may have a trial by jury (if hereafter applicable);
He is innocent until proven guilty;
He may have the complaining or material witness present and the right to cross examine;
He may bring witnesses on his own behalf.
(Comp. Ords. 2005, § 6-204)

852:
Upon a verdict or plea of guilty and after announcing the judgment, the court shall impose sentence. The court may:
Impose a fine, with or without a commitment for confinement;
Commit such person for confinement;
Sentence such person to community service work;
Suspend the execution of the sentence in whole or in part;
Defer the execution of the sentence or any portion or portions thereof to one or more fixed dates in the future.
(Comp. Ords. 2005, § 6-212)

885:
The following types of signs are prohibited throughout the city:
Roof signs;
Streamers and other wind actuated devices (other than the ones described in section 28-10);
Signs on the public right-of-way other than publicly owned or maintained signs;
Window signs which exceed 30 percent of the window area;
Signs which contain words, pictures, or statements which are obscene, as defined by the Official Code of Georgia Annotated § 16-12-80;
Signs which simulate an official traffic control or warning sign or hide from view any traffic or street sign, signal or public service sign;
Signs which emit or utilize in any manner any sound capable of being detected on any traveled road or highway by a person with normal hearing;
Signs which interfere with road or highway visibility or obstruct or otherwise interfere with the safe and orderly movement of traffic; and
Signs erected by nailing, fastening or affixing the sign in any manner to any tree, post, curve, utility pole, or other structure except as set forth herein;
No sign shall be allowed which rotates at a rate exceeding six revolutions per minute.
Signs which advertise an activity which is illegal under Georgia or Federal Laws or Regulations or by the Georgia Department of Transportation.
Signs which obstruct any fire escape, any means of egress or ventilation, or which prevents free passage from one part of a roof to any other part thereof;
No sign shall be attached in any manner to a fire escape.
Signs that are not maintained and are not in good repair are in violation of the City of Dallas codes. Examples of signs that would be in violation are signs that contain or exhibit broken panels, visible rust, visible rot, damaged support structures, or missing letters.
Abandoned signs.
(Ord. No. OA-2013-03, 4-1-2013)

940:
Utility permits shall be obtained from the director (or such other person as the city manager may designate) upon application made on forms prescribed by the city. The written application shall include the following:
The name and address of the utility;
The nature, extent, and location of any work proposed to be done, along with satisfactory plans as attachments showing in detail the location of the proposed facility or operations as described in the permit application. The plans shall show the size or capacity of facilities to be installed; their relationship to street features such as right-of-way lines, pavement edge, structures, etc., horizontal and vertical clearance to critical elements of the roadway and any other information necessary to evaluate the impact on the street and its operation;
The name and address of the person or firm who is to do such work;
The name, street address, email address if applicable and telephone and facsimile numbers of one or more facilities representative(s);
The projected dates for the work to be started and finished;
An indemnity bond, cash bond or other acceptable security which type of bond or security shall be selected by the city and in an amount to be set by the city to pay any damages to any part of the city road system or other city property or to any city employee or member of the public caused by activity or work of the utility performed under authority of the permit issued;
A copy, if requested, of the registrant's certificate of authority (or other acceptable evidence of authority to operate) from the Georgia Public Service Commission and/or the FCC and any other similar approvals, permits, or agreements; and
A copy, if requested, of the service agreement, if applicable or other legal instrument that authorizes the utility to use or occupy the right-of-way for the purpose described in the application.
(Ord. No. OA-2016-03, § 1, 7-11-2016)

1004:
The public health, safety, morals and general welfare require that the harmonious, orderly and progressive development of land within the city. In furtherance of this purpose, the regulations from which this article is derived are adopted to:
Encourage the development of economically sound and stable communities;
Ensure the provision of required streets, and where practical, utilities and other facilities and services to new land development;
Ensure the adequate provision of safe and convenient traffic access and circulation, both vehicular and pedestrian, in new land development;
Ensure the provision of needed open space and building sites in new land developments, through the dedication or reservation of land for recreational, educational and other public purposes;
Ensure, in general, the wise development of new areas, in harmony with the comprehensive plan of the community as it now exists or may hereafter be established.
(Ord. No. OA-2023-06, § I, 10-9-2023)
